  "theorem_name": "FixedPeriodicMimicryInEverySufficientlyLargeDyadicBlock",
  "declared_proposition": "Fix M>=1, a modulo M with gcd(a,M)=gcd(a+2,M)=1, and any feature F periodic modulo M. Choose a prime ell not dividing 2M and let r be the reduced CRT class r=a modulo M, r=-2 modulo ell. Then there is X_0 such that every dyadic interval [X,2X] with X>=X_0 contains a prime p congruent to r modulo M ell. Consequently F(p,p+2)=F(a,a+2) while p+2 is composite in every sufficiently large dyadic block.",
  "mathematical_argument": "CRT gives gcd(r,M ell)=1. The prime number theorem in arithmetic progressions for the fixed modulus Q=M ell gives pi(2X;Q,r)-pi(X;Q,r) asymptotic to X/(phi(Q) log X), hence positive for every sufficiently large X. Taking X_0>ell makes ell a proper divisor of p+2, and periodicity preserves F.",
  "result_classification": "partial_theorem",
  "problem_status": "open_not_proven",
